-- DICT-SEMANTICS §4 (`gen`) / §4.2 (obligation deferral): an INFERRED binding
-- whose residual predicate carries a GROUND argument must keep that argument in
-- the deferred obligation, so the call site is checked against the WHOLE vector
-- and not against the tyvar-shaped half of it.
--
-- REGRESSION GUARD FOR #1905 (inferred binding drops ground-arg predicate), S0,
-- fixed by sprint/entailment-verdict. No `must_fail` pin ever existed for this
-- one, so before this row the S0 had no guard in the tree at all.
--
-- SPEC ANSWER (hand-derived from §3/§4, NOT captured). `useIx x = ix x 'z'` has
-- no signature, so §4 `gen` generalises it over the residual predicate
-- `Ix α Char` -- the second argument is GROUND (`Char`, forced by the literal
-- `'z'`), the first is the generalised tyvar. `main` instantiates it at
-- `α := Char`, giving the goal `Ix Char Char`. §3's matching set is ONE φ
-- against the WHOLE vector: the program declares only `impl Ix Int Char`, and
-- φ would need `Char = Int`. The set is EMPTY. THE PROGRAM IS REJECTED,
-- T-NO-IMPL, `No impl of Ix for Char Char`, on check, run AND build.
--
-- MEASURED AT THE PRE-SPRINT ARM `264eb95d` (this tree, cold-built): check 0,
-- run 0, build 0, and THE SHIPPED BINARY RAN TO COMPLETION PRINTING 222 -- the
-- body of `impl Ix Int Char`, reached from a goal whose first argument is
-- `Char`. Every verb green, every verb wrong. The `Char` in the predicate was
-- dropped on the way into the deferred obligation, so the call site compared
-- one argument's worth of goal against a two-argument impl and matched.
--
-- ⚠️ THE ROW'S REJECT VERDICT IS ONLY HALF ITS VALUE; THE OTHER HALF IS THAT
-- 222 CANNOT COME BACK. `ALL_EXACT` is unavailable to a REJECT row, so the code
-- cell (`T-NO-IMPL`) is what distinguishes "rejected for the reason the spec
-- gives" from "rejected because the fixture has a typo" -- without it a future
-- unrelated breakage in this file would grade green.
--
-- ⚠️ ONE impl block, so Section 4 derives no permutation pair here, correctly:
-- the defect was ACCEPTANCE of an unsatisfiable goal, not declaration order.
-- The order axis for a two-argument `Ix` is carried by
-- s3-nary-requires-goal-vector.mdk and s-cardinality-same-iface2.mdk.
interface Ix a b where
  ix : a -> b -> Int

impl Ix Int Char where
  ix _ _ = 222

useIx x = ix x 'z'

main = println (useIx 'q')
